{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "7",
    "case_summary": "An 11-year-old boy presented with progressive unilateral skin hardening on his right thigh and buttock for 7 years. Recently, the motion of his right limb joint was slightly limited. He was born to healthy parents and did not have any medical disease.\n\nThere was an ill-defined, stony-hard plaque involving his right thigh above the patella extending to his right buttocks with loss of skin elasticity. The affected skin was associated with mild hypertrichosis and hyperpigmentation.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Normal results for routine blood tests, autoantibodies, immunoglobulins, C-reactive protein, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate.- Imaging studies:  Image Title: MRI of right thigh  Image Description: Demonstrated slight thickening of subcutaneous tissue and T2 signal abnormality- Pathological examination:  - Histopathological findings: Normal epidermis and mild pigmentation along the basal layer with subtle increase in density of fibroblasts extending to the subcutis and forming adipocyte entrapment but no evidence of inflammation. Dense reticular dermis extended below well-preserved appendages  - Immunohistochemical staining: Increased expression of CD34 in the reticular dermis  - Alcian blue staining: Negative\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Stiff skin syndrome\nB. Segmental stiff skin syndrome (SSS)\nC. Linear Scleroderma (Morphea)\nD. Congenital Fascial Dystrophy (Stiff Skin Syndrome)\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Chronic Progressive Unilateral Lower-Limb Dermatological Deficit with Subcutaneous Tissue Involvement",
      "time_course": "chronic",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "progressive unilateral skin hardening",
        "limited motion of right limb joint",
        "mild hypertrichosis and hyperpigmentation",
        "MRI showing slight thickening of subcutaneous tissue and T2 signal abnormality"
